  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(75.624pt, 177.98pt, "Aston Institute of Photonic Technologies, Aston University, B4 7ET, Birmingham, UK ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 6.48pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 192.26pt, "b")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(75.624pt, 195.26pt, "Bruker Nano GmbH, Am Studio 2D, 12489 Berlin, Germany ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 9.96pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 231.5pt, "Abstract. ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(118.1pt, 231.5pt, "We show that with the use of a short wavelength (520nm) femtosecond laser source, Two-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 248.69pt, "Photon Polymerization (2PP) fabrication of micro-membrane designs with channel sizes down to 1 μm ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 265.97pt, "is possible, using commercial photopolymers OrmoComp and FemtoBond. This approach, involving ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 283.25pt, "the flexible design and direct manufacturing of micro-filters and micro-membranes, can be applied for ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 300.41pt, "novel microfluidic devices. ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 9.96pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 338.45pt, "Keywords: ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(125.78pt, 338.45pt, "two-photon polymerization,")
  #line-at(248.21pt, 338.45pt, "micro-membranes, microfluidics. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 376.37pt, "*Vladimir Osipov, ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#467886"))
  #line-at(150.74pt, 376.37pt, "v.osipov@aston.ac.uk")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(75.384pt, 412.75pt, "1 Introduction.")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#241d38"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 433.51pt, "The modern field of microfluidics focuses on the precise control and manipulation of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 454.15pt, "liquids in very small volumes. Microfluidic devices are especially important for lab-on-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 474.91pt, "a-chip applications for biomedical, chemical or physical studies. To investigate ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 495.55pt, "phenomena occurring at a small scale, microfluidic channels are enhanced by the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 516.31pt, "integration of even smaller elements, including active and passive components,  such ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 536.95pt, "as filters, membranes, valves or mixers.  However, conventional fabrication ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 557.71pt, "techniques for microfluidic chips have been largely limited to planar structures, holding ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 578.38pt, "back the exploitation of three-dimensional architectures, e.g. for multi-phase droplet ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 599.14pt, "separation or wet-phase fiber spinning Ref.1. Modern designs of microfluidic chips ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 619.78pt, "follow the “organ-on-chip” approach, which includes versatile 3D-positioning of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 640.54pt, "microfluidic components, which are difficult to realize using conventional ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 661.18pt, "manufacturing techniques. ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(216.05pt, 661.18pt, "Understanding the nature of the transport process and the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 681.94pt, "precise evaluation of relevant parameters (especially size and porous 3D geometry) ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 702.58pt, "are of fundamental and practical interest Ref.2. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 723.34pt, "Polymeric membranes are widely used in various microfluidic chip designs, mainly due ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 744pt, "to their high performance. Thin membranes are particularly difficult to handle because ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 764.76pt, "the risk of tearing increases with the density and size of pores in the membrane. That ")
]

// arXiv source page 2
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 83.3pt, "is why many fabrication protocols involve molds fabricated of more solid material. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 103.94pt, "However molds typically require pretreatment or the application of sacrificial layers to ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 124.7pt, "prevent membrane adhesion to the mold, which is usually hydrophilic Ref.3.  ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 145.34pt, "The main purpose of this work is to demonstrate the capability of the fs laser based ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 166.1pt, "2PP technique for micro-membrane design and direct fabrication of channels with ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 186.74pt, "sizes down to 1 micron, enabling application in microfluidic systems for microparticle ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 207.5pt, "or microbiological object separation. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 234.14pt, "The 2PP laser fabrication technique, Ref.4, allows for direct fabrication of designed ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 254.93pt, "micro-scaffolds and porous-like micro-structures with micron or sub-micron scale ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 275.57pt, "precision: this is a radical and flexible concept that is not achievable by established ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 296.33pt, "additive manufacturing techniques and which leads to a range of novel device ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 316.97pt, "possibilities. For example, 2PP has been used to fabricate a set of compact optical ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 337.73pt, "components (lenses, waveguides, gratings etc) and can be used to create the micro-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 358.37pt, "fluidic structure of a device incorporating precision mechanical components such as ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 379.13pt, "locks and shutters. It has previously been used for the fabrication of biomedical micro-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 399.77pt, "optics for laser surgery Ref.5, three-focus intraocular lens design Ref.6, drug delivery ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 420.55pt, "Ref.7 and intra-vascular pressure sensing Ref.8. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 447.19pt, "Recently, 2PP lithography, was employed to print artificial channels of 4 µm in diameter ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 467.95pt, "Ref.9. The channel was printed into a traffic-cone-shaped structure including a needle ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 488.59pt, "shaped void for a traditional needle electrode to sit in. The needle was glued to the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 509.35pt, "cone and then cast into an epoxy resin cube to form a needle-plane geometry with 2 ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 529.99pt, "mm insulation distance. ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 571.42pt, "2 Methods")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 598.18pt, "2.1 Design ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 618.82pt, "We ")
  #line-at(103.68pt, 618.82pt, "used")
  #line-at(143.54pt, 618.82pt, "COMSOL Multiphysics")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(264.29pt, 614.86pt, "®")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(270.17pt, 618.82pt, " 5.4 ")
  #line-at(303.986pt, 618.82pt, "software ")
  #line-at(363.098pt, 618.82pt, "to ")
  #line-at(386.954pt, 618.82pt, "design ")
  #line-at(436.15pt, 618.82pt, "our ")
  #line-at(467.314pt, 618.82pt, "structures. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 639.58pt, "COMSOL Multiphysics")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(192.77pt, 635.62pt, "®")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(198.65pt, 639.58pt, " is a simulation platform that provides fully coupled multi-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 660.22pt, "physics and single-physics modelling capabilities. This platform includes all the steps ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 680.98pt, "in the modelling workflow — from defining geometries, material properties, and the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 701.62pt, "physics that describe specific phenomena to performing computations and evaluating ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 722.38pt, "the results. Models deloped in COMSOL can be easily exported in stl-file format, that ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 743.04pt, "can be used for fabrication using a 3D printer or similar manufacturing device, such as ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 763.8pt, "our 2PP system.  ")
]

// arXiv source page 3
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-03.png", width: 446.5pt), 77pt, 347pt))[
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 83.3pt, "2.2. 2PP setup ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 103.94pt, "Aston Institute of Photonic Technologies has designed and built a custom 2PP system, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 124.7pt, "which is based around a Chromacity Spark1040 laser (wavelength 520 nm, repetition ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 145.34pt, "rate 100 MHz, pulse length 120fs, average power 10 mW), LaserNanoFab galvano-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 166.1pt, "scanner with controller and 3D nano-positioner with travel range 5mm, operated by ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 186.74pt, "M4D software. For the work described here, we used a GT Vision dry 60x objective ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 207.5pt, "with NA of 0.8. Scanning speed was 0.25 mm/s with slicing 500nm and hatching 250 ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 228.14pt, "nm. The ability to use 520 nm laser wavelength was chosen to enable a reduction in ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 248.93pt, "the sizes of fabricated microstructures in comparison with popular 780 nm laser ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 269.57pt, "radiation Ref.10.  It has so far been tested on 3D micro-structure fabrication with SU-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 290.33pt, "8, FemtoBond and Ormocomp photopolymers. The construction of the sample holder ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 310.97pt, "(Fig.1) allows 2PP fabrication processing of polymer films on both planar glass ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 331.73pt, "substrates or optical fibre-top end facet with precision about 200 nm.   ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 9.96pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(230.45pt, 408.19pt, "Fig.1")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(253.73pt, 408.19pt, " Flexible 2PP fabrication facilities with possibility to process ")
  #line-at(230.45pt, 425.47pt, "glass substrates or 125 μm diameter optical fiber end facets.  ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 503.95pt, "3 Results ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 524.71pt, "The image of a proposed 3D design of micro-membrane with 95 x 95 x 5μm-size and ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 545.35pt, "with 10, 8, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 μm size square holes is shown in Fig.2a and an optical ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 566.11pt, "microscopy view of 2PP-fabricated FemtoBond polymer sample is shown in Fig.2b.  ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 9.96pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 726.82pt, "Fig.2.")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(98.064pt, 726.82pt, " a) Proposed 3D design of micro-membrane; b) 95 x 95 x 5μm-size 2PP-fabricated FemtoBond ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 744.12pt, "sample with 10, 8, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1μm size square holes on glass substrate, c) example of membrane-")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 761.28pt, "based sensor on optical fiber top in ferrule. ")
]

